982
00:36:46,938 --> 00:36:48,272
Just blend them.

983
00:36:49,207 --> 00:36:50,741
The potatoes aren't cooked.

984
00:36:50,808 --> 00:36:54,712
Eun-yeong will have to give up
on the potatoes and just use radish.

985
00:36:54,779 --> 00:36:57,848
-One minute.
-One minute left.

986
00:36:59,317 --> 00:37:00,918
MASHED POTATOES ON THE JJAJANG RICE!

987
00:37:00,985 --> 00:37:03,254
-Do you need the potatoes?
-Add oil as you work.

988
00:37:03,321 --> 00:37:04,889
It's an emergency.

989
00:37:04,956 --> 00:37:06,023
-A serious emergency.
-Oh, no.

990
00:37:06,224 --> 00:37:08,025
STICKING RADISH LEAVES IN

991
00:37:08,092 --> 00:37:09,961
-Just cover it up. Potatoes?
-Hurry up.

992
00:37:10,027 --> 00:37:12,763
We might see the two worst dishes
of the show at the same time.

993
00:37:14,732 --> 00:37:17,668
-Thirty seconds left.
-Thirty seconds!

994
00:37:17,735 --> 00:37:18,769
Thirty seconds!

995
00:37:19,270 --> 00:37:22,106
HE'S DRAWING SOMETHING
AT THIS POINT IN TIME?

996
00:37:23,908 --> 00:37:26,110
-What's that?
-It doesn't look good.

997
00:37:26,177 --> 00:37:27,745
-What's that?
-Balsamic sauce.

998
00:37:27,812 --> 00:37:28,946
-Balsamic?
-Yes.

999
00:37:29,013 --> 00:37:30,348
BALSAMIC SAUCE?

1000
00:37:31,148 --> 00:37:33,484
-There's the dinosaur.
-It's like a comic book.

1001
00:37:33,884 --> 00:37:35,920
THE DINOSAUR APPEARS

1002
00:37:35,987 --> 00:37:37,722
-Wait.
-What is this?

1003
00:37:37,788 --> 00:37:39,156
Which side is the head?

1004
00:37:39,590 --> 00:37:42,994
FOR THOSE OF YOU
WHO CAN'T FIGURE IT OUT LIKE WINTER…

1005
00:37:44,195 --> 00:37:45,696
Just like that.

1006
00:37:45,763 --> 00:37:47,832
THE REVIVAL OF A T-REX

1007
00:37:47,898 --> 00:37:49,734
The eye. Whatever. It's green onion.

1008
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:51,235
GREEN ONION EYE WITH BALSAMIC SAUCE

1009
00:37:51,302 --> 00:37:52,570
What is that?

1010
00:37:52,637 --> 00:37:54,572
The eye. He's putting something on it.

1011
00:37:55,039 --> 00:37:56,774
THE EYE IS DONE

1012
00:37:57,408 --> 00:37:59,443
WATERMELON SEED NOSTRILS

1013
00:37:59,510 --> 00:38:03,180
-The round is over!
-Time's up!

1014
00:38:03,247 --> 00:38:05,249
-It's time!
-Ring the bell.

1015
00:38:05,316 --> 00:38:07,952
You cannot make anything new.

1016
00:38:08,019 --> 00:38:09,220
Look at that.

1017
00:38:09,654 --> 00:38:12,523
MAKING TEETH WITH MUSHROOMS

1018
00:38:13,491 --> 00:38:15,393
THERE HE IS, MY STEVEN SPIELBERG

1019
00:38:15,693 --> 00:38:19,163
PRESENTING HIS DISH
IN A WAY THAT KIDS WOULD DIE FOR

1020
00:38:20,197 --> 00:38:22,233
Poong finishes.

1021
00:38:22,466 --> 00:38:24,268
PLEASED

1022
00:38:24,335 --> 00:38:26,103
-I don't think this is happening.
-Why not?

1023
00:38:26,671 --> 00:38:29,073
-The potatoes didn't work…
-The potatoes?

1024
00:38:29,140 --> 00:38:30,641
…so they won't stick.

1025
00:38:30,708 --> 00:38:33,311
You called yourself
the KARINA of Chinese cuisine for nothing.

1026
00:38:33,377 --> 00:38:37,982
Eun-yeong just needs to stick
a few more radish pieces on it.

1027
00:38:38,582 --> 00:38:39,950
CORONA WITH HER HEAD DOWN…

1028
00:38:40,017 --> 00:38:42,186
-KARINA of Chinese cuisine.
-It's done.

1029
00:38:42,253 --> 00:38:44,422
You said you were KARINA. What is this?

1030
00:38:44,488 --> 00:38:45,589
A jellyfish.

1031
00:38:45,656 --> 00:38:46,857
A tomb?

1032
00:38:46,924 --> 00:38:50,061
That's all. Chef Eun-yeong finishes too!

1033
00:38:50,127 --> 00:38:51,996
PARK EUN-YEONG'S ARMAGEPORK IS DONE

1034
00:38:52,697 --> 00:38:53,831
THE 5-MINUTE CHECK IS DONE TOO

1035
00:38:53,898 --> 00:38:55,232
-That's the last bit.
-Whoa.

1036
00:38:55,299 --> 00:38:56,701
-It's convincing.
-It's like a T-Rex.

1037
00:38:57,001 --> 00:38:59,203
T-REX IS ON ITS WAY

1038
00:38:59,270 --> 00:39:00,338
Its arm is kind of long.

1039
00:39:02,306 --> 00:39:05,843
DISHES FROM COMICS ARE ON THEIR WAY!

1040
00:39:05,910 --> 00:39:07,511
Both dishes are finished.

1041
00:39:07,578 --> 00:39:09,046
THE TOUGHEST COOK-OFF YET

1042
00:39:09,113 --> 00:39:10,848
That was the toughest cook-off yet.

1043
00:39:11,782 --> 00:39:15,219
-We must give it to you.
-They had to focus on the visuals.

1044
00:39:15,286 --> 00:39:18,789
Both dishes were finished for KARINA.

1045
00:39:20,825 --> 00:39:22,426
As you can see,

1046
00:39:22,493 --> 00:39:23,728
it's a female dinosaur.

1047
00:39:23,794 --> 00:39:25,596
-How… What?
-I see. The lashes.

1048
00:39:25,663 --> 00:39:28,099
-I drew the lashes in.
-The eyelashes.

1049
00:39:28,165 --> 00:39:30,668
I wanted to make you
the main character of a cooking comic,

1050
00:39:30,735 --> 00:39:32,436
as you always should be.

1051
00:39:32,503 --> 00:39:35,906
-I woke up as a T-Rex?
-Yes.

1052
00:39:35,973 --> 00:39:37,174
I made you the main character.

1053
00:39:37,241 --> 00:39:39,276
-That's a loss for me.
-A dinosaur,

1054
00:39:39,343 --> 00:39:41,412
-but a girl.
-Yes, it's a female dinosaur.

1055
00:39:41,479 --> 00:39:44,882
Please try Poong's dish first.

1056
00:39:45,182 --> 00:39:49,587
A DINOSAUR DUMPLING
THAT'LL CAPTURE KARINA'S INTEREST

1057
00:39:50,654 --> 00:39:53,991
WEBTOON ARTIST KIM POONG'S KARINASAUR

1058
00:39:54,258 --> 00:39:55,526
EYE CONTACT

1059
00:39:55,593 --> 00:39:58,362
It's just the head.
Take a good look up close.

1060
00:39:58,429 --> 00:39:59,697
It's convincing.

1061
00:40:00,598 --> 00:40:04,869
You woke up and realized
you've become a T-Rex. That's it.

1062
00:40:04,935 --> 00:40:07,037
-Karinasaur.
-It's cute.

1063
00:40:07,104 --> 00:40:09,407
So where do I begin?

1064
00:40:09,473 --> 00:40:11,208
It doesn't matter where.

1065
00:40:11,275 --> 00:40:12,643
-It's all the same.
-Grab it.

1066
00:40:12,710 --> 00:40:14,412
-Just help yourself.
-But how?

1067
00:40:14,478 --> 00:40:16,013
The cheeks are delicious, right?

1068
00:40:16,080 --> 00:40:18,115
-The cheeks should be good.
-It's probably good.

1069
00:40:18,182 --> 00:40:19,784
I think the arm should be good.

1070
00:40:19,850 --> 00:40:20,851
Like <i>jokbal</i>.

1071
00:40:21,118 --> 00:40:23,187
CHECKING THE CUT

1072
00:40:23,387 --> 00:40:24,255
WAY TOO HARD

1073
00:40:24,321 --> 00:40:25,322
-It's…
-Like <i>jokbal</i>.

1074
00:40:26,457 --> 00:40:27,658
-I can't eat this.
-You can't?

1075
00:40:27,725 --> 00:40:29,560
-It's just bones.
-The legs should be good, not the arms.

1076
00:40:29,627 --> 00:40:31,695
Let me try this part.

1077
00:40:31,762 --> 00:40:33,597
-It's like <i>sujebi</i>.
-The cheeks are chubby.

1078
00:40:33,664 --> 00:40:35,833
-You put the filling in, right?
-Yes.

1079
00:40:35,900 --> 00:40:38,803
-Everywhere else has the filling inside.
-Dinosaur meat is red too?

1080
00:40:38,869 --> 00:40:42,139
-Because when you cut open a dinosaur…
-You're right.

1081
00:40:42,206 --> 00:40:43,741
-…it should be red.
-Yes.

1082
00:40:44,074 --> 00:40:45,776
CAPTURING EVERYONE'S ATTENTION

1083
00:40:45,843 --> 00:40:48,245
It would've been better
if ketchup shot out.

1084
00:40:48,312 --> 00:40:49,480
It's a shame.

1085
00:40:49,780 --> 00:40:50,614
HESITANT

1086
00:40:50,681 --> 00:40:52,450
I don't know why, but I feel bad.

1087
00:40:52,516 --> 00:40:55,619
KARINA is devouring the dinosaur.

1088
00:40:55,686 --> 00:40:59,089
You're now watching
KARINA feeding on a dinosaur.

1089
00:40:59,156 --> 00:41:01,659
That's the neck area.

1090
00:41:01,725 --> 00:41:03,394
THE GENRE OF THIS COMIC BOOK IS HORROR

1091
00:41:03,461 --> 00:41:05,763
-She's the first to eat a dinosaur.
-This is so odd.

1092
00:41:05,830 --> 00:41:08,766
Get some balsamic sauce on the dish
before you take a bite.

1093
00:41:08,833 --> 00:41:11,168
-This?
-She's supposed to eat that too?

1094
00:41:11,235 --> 00:41:13,270
-The sauce was there for a reason.
-Yes.

1095
00:41:13,737 --> 00:41:14,972
HESITANT

1096
00:41:15,039 --> 00:41:16,474
-I'll eat it now.
-Okay.

1097
00:41:16,540 --> 00:41:18,142
-You got this.
-She's eating a dinosaur.

1098
00:41:18,209 --> 00:41:19,510
SUDDENLY BECOMES A MUKBANG CHALLENGE

1099
00:41:19,577 --> 00:41:20,811
-You got this.
-You got this.

1100
00:41:21,312 --> 00:41:23,681
I wonder what dinosaur cheek is like.

1101
00:41:25,349 --> 00:41:27,051
CHEWS CAREFULLY

1102
00:41:27,117 --> 00:41:28,352
MMM

1103
00:41:28,986 --> 00:41:30,754
CHUCKLING

1104
00:41:30,821 --> 00:41:33,090
-Wait a second.
-So?

1105
00:41:33,157 --> 00:41:34,558
Is she trying to spit it out?

1106
00:41:34,625 --> 00:41:35,626
Do you need a tissue?

1107
00:41:36,594 --> 00:41:38,128
-Hmm?
-Yes?

1108
00:41:38,195 --> 00:41:39,263
-Must be good.
-Is it?

1109
00:41:39,330 --> 00:41:40,998
WHAT'S IT LIKE?

1110
00:41:41,065 --> 00:41:44,568
-It tastes like <i>jeyuk-bokkeum</i>.
-The spicy pork stir-fry?

1111
00:41:44,635 --> 00:41:45,636
Dinosaur <i>jeyuk-bokkeum</i>?

1112
00:41:45,703 --> 00:41:47,271
Dinosaur <i>jeyuk-bokkeum</i>?

1113
00:41:47,838 --> 00:41:49,240
-Did you use meat?
-Yes.

1114
00:41:49,306 --> 00:41:51,575
<i>I mixed pork neck and shrimp.</i>

1115
00:41:52,576 --> 00:41:54,345
She's trying the muscle now.

1116
00:41:54,411 --> 00:41:56,146
-The muscle?
-It's good.

1117
00:41:56,213 --> 00:41:57,181
-It's good?
-It's there?

1118
00:41:57,248 --> 00:41:59,149
It's the color too.

1119
00:41:59,216 --> 00:42:00,050
GASP!

1120
00:42:00,117 --> 00:42:01,886
What are the teeth like?

1121
00:42:01,952 --> 00:42:04,288
Don't eat the teeth.

1122
00:42:04,355 --> 00:42:06,991
-They're raw mushrooms.
-You can't eat the teeth anyway.

1123
00:42:07,057 --> 00:42:08,759
I doubt people ate dinosaur teeth.

1124
00:42:10,361 --> 00:42:12,096
Are these watermelon seeds?

1125
00:42:12,162 --> 00:42:14,431
Yes, I made the nostrils
with watermelon seeds.

1126
00:42:14,698 --> 00:42:15,833
MAXIMUM LEVEL ON CREATIVITY

1127
00:42:15,900 --> 00:42:19,270
-One fell, so let me put it back.
-One of them dropped.

1128
00:42:19,336 --> 00:42:22,206
-You're right.
-It's just clogged up.

1129
00:42:22,273 --> 00:42:23,407
One of them fell.

1130
00:42:23,474 --> 00:42:24,608
-The skin.
-I'll be brave.

1131
00:42:24,675 --> 00:42:26,510
Dinosaurs are reptiles, right?

1132
00:42:26,577 --> 00:42:28,879
I'll try the arm.

1133
00:42:28,946 --> 00:42:29,947
There's no filling inside.

1134
00:42:30,014 --> 00:42:32,149
-It's probably bad.
-There's no filling in here?

1135
00:42:32,883 --> 00:42:34,118
A BITE OF THE ARM OUT OF CURIOSITY

1136
00:42:34,184 --> 00:42:35,986
You can always spit it out.

1137
00:42:37,721 --> 00:42:40,090
GIGGLING

1138
00:42:40,157 --> 00:42:42,593
-Not all cuts can be delicious.
-You're right.

1139
00:42:42,660 --> 00:42:45,195
-I'm sure there are good cuts.
-Like legs.

1140
00:42:45,262 --> 00:42:46,196
MMM

1141
00:42:46,263 --> 00:42:48,666
You know what? It's good.

1142
00:42:48,732 --> 00:42:49,833
-It is?
-Yes.

1143
00:42:49,900 --> 00:42:50,801
The filling's good, right?

1144
00:42:50,868 --> 00:42:53,003
It's like <i>jeyuk-bokkeum</i> over rice.

1145
00:42:53,070 --> 00:42:54,138
WHOA

1146
00:42:54,204 --> 00:42:56,707
-That's a success.
-You said you loved Korean food,

1147
00:42:56,774 --> 00:42:59,276
-so I took that route.
-It's intentional, right?

1148
00:42:59,343 --> 00:43:01,412
That dinosaur
must've eaten a pig before it died.

1149
00:43:01,679 --> 00:43:03,047
LOL

1150
00:43:03,113 --> 00:43:05,349
I'm sure dinosaurs tasted
something like <i>jeyuk-bokkeum</i>.

1151
00:43:07,017 --> 00:43:08,519
We'll try to imagine that.

1152
00:43:08,586 --> 00:43:11,055
The dinosaur wasn't wild.

1153
00:43:11,121 --> 00:43:13,257
-Was it farmed?
-It was farmed

1154
00:43:13,324 --> 00:43:15,893
and was often fed with pigs,
so it smells like it.

1155
00:43:15,960 --> 00:43:17,995
-While it was farmed?
-Farmed?

1156
00:43:18,062 --> 00:43:19,396
Did you find our conversation fun?

1157
00:43:19,463 --> 00:43:20,531
THAT'S RIGHT

1158
00:43:20,598 --> 00:43:21,999
WANTED TO BE INCLUDED

1159
00:43:22,066 --> 00:43:23,467
Just let me join in.

1160
00:43:24,635 --> 00:43:26,737
In the end, I ate myself, right?

1161
00:43:27,037 --> 00:43:29,006
THE AUTHOR'S INTENT?

1162
00:43:29,073 --> 00:43:29,974
Is that the case?

1163
00:43:30,040 --> 00:43:32,743
-It must be a horror comic.
-So I was a T-Rex in a previous life,

1164
00:43:32,810 --> 00:43:35,679
-and I ate myself in this life.
-That's right.

1165
00:43:35,746 --> 00:43:38,382
I honestly like this story.

1166
00:43:38,749 --> 00:43:40,417
COULD BE A MASTERPIECE

1167
00:43:41,018 --> 00:43:42,620
Now, next,

1168
00:43:42,686 --> 00:43:44,188
the work in question.

1169
00:43:44,254 --> 00:43:46,690
-"In question"?
-Chef Eun-yeong's dish.

1170
00:43:46,757 --> 00:43:49,660
A WHITE RADISH TREE
RIGHT OUT OF A COMIC BOOK

1171
00:43:50,361 --> 00:43:53,497
FILLED WITH DARK JJAJANG RICE

1172
00:43:54,665 --> 00:43:58,535
CHEF PARK EUN-YEONG'S ARMAGEPORK

1173
00:43:58,602 --> 00:44:01,405
-Wait, it's really pretty.
-Isn't it?

1174
00:44:01,472 --> 00:44:04,008
She used about two more minutes

1175
00:44:04,074 --> 00:44:06,810
trying to stick those in the potato.

1176
00:44:06,877 --> 00:44:11,982
So take that into consideration
before you make a decision.

1177
00:44:12,049 --> 00:44:14,518
-What does it symbolize?
-A flower, right?

1178
00:44:14,585 --> 00:44:15,686
Actually,

1179
00:44:15,753 --> 00:44:18,422
if the potatoes had cooked
all the way through,

1180
00:44:18,489 --> 00:44:22,292
I wanted to add more leaves
and make the tree look fuller.

1181
00:44:22,359 --> 00:44:27,097
But what I ended up making looks like
a tree from a horror comic book.

1182
00:44:27,164 --> 00:44:30,200
After all, you also have
an image as a warrior,

1183
00:44:30,267 --> 00:44:33,303
so please imagine a warrior
sitting on that flower

1184
00:44:33,370 --> 00:44:38,375
after defeating her enemy
in a brutal action comic book.

1185
00:44:38,442 --> 00:44:40,344
She tried her best.

1186
00:44:40,411 --> 00:44:42,246
She had been thinking about it all along.

1187
00:44:42,312 --> 00:44:45,115
It looks like something from a film
than a comic book.

1188
00:44:45,182 --> 00:44:46,850
Since she's a warrior,

1189
00:44:46,917 --> 00:44:48,552
it can be the egg where aliens hatch from.

1190
00:44:48,852 --> 00:44:50,387
AN ALIEN EGG?

1191
00:44:50,454 --> 00:44:51,855
Doesn't it look like that?

1192
00:44:51,922 --> 00:44:54,425
-She put <i>jjajang </i>rice inside, right?
-Yes.

1193
00:44:54,491 --> 00:44:55,826
Once she digs it up,

1194
00:44:55,893 --> 00:44:57,394
-it'll look like dirt.
-How exciting.

1195
00:44:57,461 --> 00:45:00,230
-So how about <i>Exhuma</i>?
<i>-Exhuma</i>.

1196
00:45:00,297 --> 00:45:01,198
That's great.

1197
00:45:01,265 --> 00:45:02,766
-She's eating dirt, then.
-Not bad.

1198
00:45:02,833 --> 00:45:04,702
How will she put the story together?

1199
00:45:04,768 --> 00:45:06,203
Think of it as a tree.

1200
00:45:06,270 --> 00:45:08,105
-How should I eat this? Just…
-Cut it in half?

1201
00:45:08,172 --> 00:45:10,641
Just tear it up and eat it.

1202
00:45:10,708 --> 00:45:13,343
LIKE HOW MY HEART IS TORN…

1203
00:45:13,410 --> 00:45:14,578
On top of the <i>jjajang</i> rice…

1204
00:45:14,945 --> 00:45:16,413
TEARING DOWN THE RADISH DISH

1205
00:45:16,613 --> 00:45:18,382
JJAJANG RICE SHOWS ITSELF

1206
00:45:18,449 --> 00:45:20,250
That <i>jjajang </i>rice was really good.

1207
00:45:20,484 --> 00:45:22,086
AGREED

1208
00:45:22,152 --> 00:45:25,189
It's like that old film
with the splitting grave, <i>Public Cemetery</i>.

1209
00:45:25,255 --> 00:45:27,458
I told you so. It looks like a grave.

1210
00:45:27,524 --> 00:45:29,426
-It feels like exhumation.
-You're right.

1211
00:45:29,493 --> 00:45:32,396
-Go with <i>Korean Ghost Stories</i>.
-<i>Korean Ghost Stories</i>.

1212
00:45:32,463 --> 00:45:33,831
Korean Jjajang Story.

1213
00:45:33,897 --> 00:45:34,898
She's trying to eat dirt!

1214
00:45:35,833 --> 00:45:36,967
"Eat dirt"?

1215
00:45:37,034 --> 00:45:38,969
She's like,
"Do I look like KARINA to you?"

1216
00:45:39,303 --> 00:45:41,772
DOES IT TASTE AS TERRIFYING AS IT LOOKS?

1217
00:45:42,005 --> 00:45:43,107
HEHE

1218
00:45:43,173 --> 00:45:44,875
-It's good.
-Is it?

1219
00:45:44,942 --> 00:45:46,276
-Why is she crying?
-<i>Jjajang </i>rice.

1220
00:45:46,343 --> 00:45:47,544
Must be sorrowfully delicious.

1221
00:45:48,545 --> 00:45:49,913
Sorrowfully delicious?

1222
00:45:49,980 --> 00:45:53,217
There's a grudge, like a nine-tailed fox
who passed away unfortunately.

1223
00:45:53,283 --> 00:45:54,318
HEHEHEHE

1224
00:45:54,384 --> 00:45:55,853
It passed away unfairly.

1225
00:45:55,919 --> 00:45:57,654
The story is forming as we speak.

1226
00:45:57,988 --> 00:45:59,523
IMMEDIATELY HAS ANOTHER BITE

1227
00:45:59,890 --> 00:46:01,425
MMM

1228
00:46:01,658 --> 00:46:02,960
HAPPY TO SEE HER

1229
00:46:03,026 --> 00:46:04,328
Are they good together?

1230
00:46:04,394 --> 00:46:05,262
Yes.

1231
00:46:05,329 --> 00:46:07,064
It goes well with the potato.

1232
00:46:07,131 --> 00:46:10,300
The aftertaste of the radish
leaves bitterness in my mouth,

1233
00:46:10,367 --> 00:46:12,269
and that's quite delicious.

1234
00:46:12,503 --> 00:46:14,238
RELIEVED

1235
00:46:14,304 --> 00:46:16,073
Is the potato undercooked?

1236
00:46:16,140 --> 00:46:19,042
-Let me try just the potato.
-Yes, just the potato.

1237
00:46:19,109 --> 00:46:20,444
YUM

1238
00:46:20,511 --> 00:46:22,780
She dug it up like it's a grave.

1239
00:46:22,846 --> 00:46:24,581
-The potato.
-Al dente.

1240
00:46:24,648 --> 00:46:26,784
LET'S SAY THAT IT'S AL DENTE

1241
00:46:26,850 --> 00:46:29,386
-Nice job.
-She added that texture intentionally.

1242
00:46:29,453 --> 00:46:31,588
But it's <i>Korean Ghost Stories</i>,

1243
00:46:31,655 --> 00:46:34,558
-so it goes well with the theme.
-It goes well?

1244
00:46:34,625 --> 00:46:36,126
Yes, even the texture.

1245
00:46:36,193 --> 00:46:37,895
Even the texture.

1246
00:46:37,961 --> 00:46:40,898
It's time to make a decision.

1247
00:46:41,498 --> 00:46:43,167
The dinosaur versus the nine-tailed fox.

1248
00:46:43,233 --> 00:46:44,468
Take one more look.

1249
00:46:44,535 --> 00:46:47,304
The dinosaur versus the nine-tailed fox.

1250
00:46:47,371 --> 00:46:48,238
STARING

1251
00:46:49,239 --> 00:46:51,608
Press one to choose Park Eun-yeong

1252
00:46:51,675 --> 00:46:53,710
and two to choose Kim Poong.

1253
00:46:53,777 --> 00:46:56,713
Make your decision and press the button!

1254
00:46:57,915 --> 00:46:59,683
INDECISIVE

1255
00:47:00,818 --> 00:47:02,352
I did.

1256
00:47:02,419 --> 00:47:03,420
MADE HER DECISION

1257
00:47:03,487 --> 00:47:05,322
Let's find out her decision.

1258
00:47:05,389 --> 00:47:06,523
Show us!

1259
00:47:06,590 --> 00:47:07,925
THE RESULT

1260
00:47:07,991 --> 00:47:10,727
I Woke Up
and Found Myself in Cooking Comics.

1261
00:47:10,794 --> 00:47:13,130
Eun-yeong has won
all six rounds against him.

1262
00:47:13,197 --> 00:47:15,566
Will Poong finally win against her?

1263
00:47:15,632 --> 00:47:18,836
Or will Eun-yeong keep her winning streak?

1264
00:47:18,902 --> 00:47:20,070
KARINA's choice!

1265
00:47:20,771 --> 00:47:21,839
Who was it?

1266
00:47:21,905 --> 00:47:23,407
KARINA!

1267
00:47:23,473 --> 00:47:24,675
KARINA!

1268
00:47:26,376 --> 00:47:27,544
KARINA!

1269
00:47:27,611 --> 00:47:29,746
KIM POONG WINS

1270
00:47:29,813 --> 00:47:31,348
KIM POONG'S KARINASAUR WINS

1271
00:47:31,415 --> 00:47:33,317
Karinasaur!

1272
00:47:33,383 --> 00:47:34,985
Karinasaur wins.

1273
00:47:35,052 --> 00:47:37,254
THE VICTORY ROAR

1274
00:47:37,754 --> 00:47:39,723
VENTING THE SORROW
OF HIS 6 CONSECUTIVE LOSSES

1275
00:47:39,790 --> 00:47:41,024
That's Kim Poong.

1276
00:47:41,792 --> 00:47:44,061
-For the first time…
-Really?

1277
00:47:44,127 --> 00:47:47,164
-…in two years, he wins against Eun-yeong.
-Two years?

1278
00:47:47,231 --> 00:47:48,565
FIRST WIN AGAINST EUN-YEONG IN 2 YEARS

1279
00:47:48,632 --> 00:47:50,634
-It happened.
-It was about time.

1280
00:47:51,668 --> 00:47:53,537
I love dinosaurs,

1281
00:47:54,605 --> 00:47:57,774
so I was really moved
that he made one for me,

1282
00:47:58,275 --> 00:47:59,643
and I also loved the story.

1283
00:47:59,710 --> 00:48:01,979
LOVED THE KARINASAUR'S STORY TOO

1284
00:48:02,045 --> 00:48:04,314
Honestly speaking,
which one tasted better?

1285
00:48:04,381 --> 00:48:05,749
-Did it also…
-Even as food?

1286
00:48:05,816 --> 00:48:08,318
-It also tasted great?
-It was delicious.

1287
00:48:09,186 --> 00:48:11,722
It was such a rough journey.

1288
00:48:11,788 --> 00:48:14,057
When I first met Eun-yeong,

1289
00:48:14,124 --> 00:48:16,660
I never thought
she'd become my kryptonite.

1290
00:48:16,727 --> 00:48:18,562
I think this is…

1291
00:48:18,629 --> 00:48:20,530
Is Eun-yeong crying?

1292
00:48:20,597 --> 00:48:22,499
-What's wrong? Was it rough?
-First off,

1293
00:48:22,566 --> 00:48:24,935
I feel really bad for KARINA.

1294
00:48:25,002 --> 00:48:27,271
-Don't say that.
-I could've done better.

1295
00:48:27,337 --> 00:48:30,407
I knew it the moment you said
you were the KARINA of Chinese cuisine.

1296
00:48:30,474 --> 00:48:32,409
-It's karma.
-It was a stretch.

1297
00:48:32,476 --> 00:48:34,478
-I think they asked for Corona.
-Right?

1298
00:48:34,544 --> 00:48:36,046
NOW THE CORONA OF CHINESE CUISINE

1299
00:48:36,113 --> 00:48:38,548
-I think that's what they wanted. Corona?
-They wanted a Corona.

1300
00:48:38,615 --> 00:48:42,619
Please put a star on Kim Poong, KARINA!

1301
00:48:43,420 --> 00:48:44,755
Great job.

1302
00:48:44,821 --> 00:48:46,390
-Congratulations.
-Thank you.

1303
00:48:46,456 --> 00:48:50,294
He beats Eun-yeong and takes the star.

1304
00:48:50,360 --> 00:48:52,296
It's his eighth star.

1305
00:48:52,362 --> 00:48:53,797
Karinasaur.

1306
00:48:53,864 --> 00:48:57,968
And please take
the <i>Chef & My Fridge</i> tableware too.

1307
00:48:58,568 --> 00:49:00,570
We'll take a break for 15 minutes.

1308
00:49:00,637 --> 00:49:02,339
Don't you want to try that?

1309
00:49:02,406 --> 00:49:04,107
I am a bit curious.

1310
00:49:04,174 --> 00:49:05,309
Try it.

1311
00:49:05,375 --> 00:49:06,677
WALKING OVER TO TRY IT

1312
00:49:07,010 --> 00:49:07,945
TAKING A PICTURE TOO

1313
00:49:08,011 --> 00:49:09,079
So cute.

1314
00:49:09,146 --> 00:49:10,514
-Let me try it.
-Try it.

1315
00:49:10,847 --> 00:49:13,083
TRYING ARMAGEPORK FIRST

1316
00:49:14,618 --> 00:49:15,886
YUM

1317
00:49:17,254 --> 00:49:18,255
MMM!

1318
00:49:18,689 --> 00:49:20,324
IT'S SO GOOD, SHE TAKES ANOTHER BITE

1319
00:49:20,657 --> 00:49:23,126
NEXT TARGET, KARINASAUR

1320
00:49:24,361 --> 00:49:25,929
What do you think? Isn't it good?

1321
00:49:27,631 --> 00:49:28,699
It's good.

1322
00:49:28,765 --> 00:49:29,967
WINTER'S PLEASED TOO

1323
00:49:30,867 --> 00:49:33,103
PREPARING TO COOK

1324
00:49:34,237 --> 00:49:36,173
Let's roll. One, two, three.

1325
00:49:36,707 --> 00:49:38,241
Chef, please check the location.

1326
00:49:40,010 --> 00:49:42,612
IS THE LOCATION OF THE SHEET IMPORTANT?

1327
00:49:43,380 --> 00:49:45,382
CAREFULLY STICKING IT ON

1328
00:49:46,616 --> 00:49:47,851
The tension is so high.

1329
00:49:48,218 --> 00:49:50,153
SON JONG-WON IS BACK IN HIS WORK MODE

1330
00:49:50,988 --> 00:49:53,190
We have to peel the shrimp too.
How fast can you peel two?

1331
00:49:53,256 --> 00:49:55,192
Under a minute.

1332
00:49:55,258 --> 00:49:57,194
We'll have enough time
even if we stay laid-back,

1333
00:49:57,260 --> 00:49:59,463
-so there's no need to rush things.
-Yes, sir.

1334
00:49:59,529 --> 00:50:01,665
Gosh. This will be fun.

1335
00:50:02,199 --> 00:50:05,068
THE AIR IS DIFFERENT FROM USUAL

1336
00:50:05,135 --> 00:50:09,172
The second round cook-off
with KARINA's ingredients.

1337
00:50:09,239 --> 00:50:13,310
For this team round, our chefs have
teamed up with their own right-hand men.

1338
00:50:13,377 --> 00:50:16,046
The chemistry of an old couple
who's been married for 14 years,

1339
00:50:16,113 --> 00:50:19,316
Chef Choi Hyun-suk and Chef Kim Seon-yeop.

1340
00:50:19,383 --> 00:50:21,251
The good vibes doppelgänger chemistry,

1341
00:50:21,318 --> 00:50:24,588
Chef Son Jong-won and Chef Woo Do-hee.

1342
00:50:24,654 --> 00:50:28,225
This is going to be
the most intense team round of them all.

1343
00:50:28,291 --> 00:50:29,326
This is the real deal.

1344
00:50:29,393 --> 00:50:31,795
They work together around the year,

1345
00:50:31,862 --> 00:50:34,297
-so they must have great teamwork.
-Yes.

1346
00:50:34,364 --> 00:50:36,566
-Look how happy they seem.
-Yes.

1347
00:50:36,633 --> 00:50:40,937
It's so different from the time
when they teamed up with Poong.

1348
00:50:41,004 --> 00:50:42,439
Tell me about it.

1349
00:50:42,506 --> 00:50:45,776
Chef Seon-yeop seems quite nervous.

1350
00:50:45,842 --> 00:50:47,911
-How does it feel to stand there?
-He must be.

1351
00:50:48,645 --> 00:50:51,748
As I mentioned before,

1352
00:50:51,815 --> 00:50:55,719
we'll have to make
the best dish within 15 minutes,

1353
00:50:55,786 --> 00:50:58,588
so what I'm worried about
is if Chef Hyun-suk…

1354
00:50:58,655 --> 00:51:01,058
Stop acting like you're so nice.

1355
00:51:01,124 --> 00:51:02,926
He keeps grabbing him by the collar,

1356
00:51:02,993 --> 00:51:06,029
-and Seon-yeop keeps on flinching.
-It's too staged.

1357
00:51:06,096 --> 00:51:07,898
Anyway, we'll show you

1358
00:51:07,964 --> 00:51:09,199
the best teamwork.

1359
00:51:09,499 --> 00:51:11,034
AMBITIOUS AND CONFIDENT

1360
00:51:11,101 --> 00:51:12,936
He says nice things after getting grabbed.

1361
00:51:13,003 --> 00:51:16,073
PRACTICED OVER AND OVER FOR 14 YEARS

1362
00:51:16,139 --> 00:51:18,275
Everyone is laughing and joking around,

1363
00:51:18,341 --> 00:51:22,712
but only Chef Do-hee
stands there with a straight face.

1364
00:51:22,779 --> 00:51:24,581
He has kept that face all along.

1365
00:51:24,648 --> 00:51:26,883
He never smiled, not even once.

1366
00:51:26,950 --> 00:51:30,320
HE DID SMILE IN SECRET

1367
00:51:30,387 --> 00:51:31,688
Is the show not fun enough?

1368
00:51:31,755 --> 00:51:33,223
No, it's fun, and I'm doing my best.

1369
00:51:33,290 --> 00:51:36,193
-Yes, I'm watching it all.
-He knows what's up.

1370
00:51:36,259 --> 00:51:38,728
-He hasn't even eaten.
-Why not?

1371
00:51:38,795 --> 00:51:41,531
He told him to eat just a banana,
so that was all they ate.

1372
00:51:41,598 --> 00:51:44,101
-My gosh.
-For this round.

1373
00:51:44,167 --> 00:51:45,969
What are you the most worried about?

1374
00:51:46,036 --> 00:51:50,607
I don't know if we'll be able to finish
everything we prepared in time.

1375
00:51:50,674 --> 00:51:53,910
Do you find Jong-won hard to trust?

1376
00:51:53,977 --> 00:51:56,113
-No.
-You're worried, even with Jong-won here?

1377
00:51:56,179 --> 00:51:59,549
I don't want to be someone
who'll be a nuisance, so I'll do my best.

1378
00:51:59,616 --> 00:52:03,086
-But this guy is already "someone."
-We already have someone.

1379
00:52:03,153 --> 00:52:04,688
He's already someone.

1380
00:52:04,754 --> 00:52:06,656
Chef Do-hee claimed

1381
00:52:06,723 --> 00:52:10,727
that he knows what Jong-won is thinking
from even the smallest head tilt he makes.

1382
00:52:10,794 --> 00:52:11,728
TELEPATHIC

1383
00:52:11,795 --> 00:52:13,230
-Yes.
-Really?

1384
00:52:13,296 --> 00:52:16,933
I've become used to figuring out
what he's thinking over my time with him.

1385
00:52:17,601 --> 00:52:19,736
I can always tell what he needs.

1386
00:52:19,803 --> 00:52:22,806
Chef Seon-yeop,
you can also do the same, right?

1387
00:52:22,873 --> 00:52:24,674
Try to guess what this means.

1388
00:52:25,876 --> 00:52:27,677
-Destroy Kim Poong.
-Correct.

1389
00:52:29,045 --> 00:52:31,681
-He can tell.
-"Destroy Kim Poong."

1390
00:52:32,048 --> 00:52:33,850
PLEASED

1391
00:52:33,917 --> 00:52:35,385
So 14 years does that.

1392
00:52:35,452 --> 00:52:37,754
There was an intel leak

1393
00:52:37,821 --> 00:52:40,157
from Chef Jong-won's side.

1394
00:52:40,223 --> 00:52:42,592
He made a timetable

1395
00:52:42,659 --> 00:52:45,529
-for this 15-minute cook-off.
-What?

1396
00:52:45,595 --> 00:52:47,330
-For this?
-We'll show you.

1397
00:52:47,397 --> 00:52:48,331
You took a picture?

1398
00:52:48,398 --> 00:52:50,467
-There it is.
-It's true!

1399
00:52:50,534 --> 00:52:53,837
Did you split up 15 minutes into minutes?

1400
00:52:53,904 --> 00:52:56,206
-It's in 30-second intervals.
-In 30 seconds?

1401
00:52:56,273 --> 00:52:59,075
-Thirty seconds?
-Can you stick to it?

1402
00:52:59,142 --> 00:53:00,510
I was going to make it 15 seconds,

1403
00:53:00,577 --> 00:53:04,381
but I considered the variables
and stuck with 30 seconds.

1404
00:53:04,447 --> 00:53:07,017
-That side is Woo, and that's Son?
-You're right.

1405
00:53:08,151 --> 00:53:10,187
-They each have different tasks to do.
-Wow.

1406
00:53:10,253 --> 00:53:13,056
We'll show you
what Chef Hyun-suk is working with.

1407
00:53:13,123 --> 00:53:14,991
-We have a timetable?
-You have one?

1408
00:53:15,559 --> 00:53:18,328
No, this team doesn't have one.

1409
00:53:18,395 --> 00:53:19,563
-They don't.
-They don't.

1410
00:53:19,629 --> 00:53:21,231
-Do you not have a plan?
-They don't need one.

1411
00:53:21,298 --> 00:53:23,600
It's not that.
We've worked together for a long time.

1412
00:53:23,667 --> 00:53:24,534
Once we have the recipe,

1413
00:53:24,601 --> 00:53:27,504
we can work systematically yet smoothly

1414
00:53:27,571 --> 00:53:30,473
as we both ride in the flow.

1415
00:53:30,540 --> 00:53:32,342
-In the flow.
-In the flow?

1416
00:53:32,409 --> 00:53:34,611
The flow could get blocked.

1417
00:53:34,678 --> 00:53:36,846
What then? Do you have a plan B?

1418
00:53:36,913 --> 00:53:37,781
If it gets blocked…

1419
00:53:37,847 --> 00:53:38,682
WAVES

1420
00:53:39,049 --> 00:53:40,617
SHOWS OFF HIS CHEEKY WAVES

1421
00:53:40,684 --> 00:53:43,253
-Then we'll flow to the other side.
-The other side.

1422
00:53:44,654 --> 00:53:46,656
TEAM CHOI: SHRIMP, MILK, TSUYU,
GOMTANG RAMYEON SOUP BASE, RED WINE

1423
00:53:46,723 --> 00:53:48,725
RIB EYE, WATERMELON, CHAMPAGNE,
LEMON JUICE, RUBY KIWI, AND MORE

1424
00:53:48,792 --> 00:53:52,162
The name of our dish is this.

1425
00:53:52,229 --> 00:53:53,330
KARINA,

1426
00:53:53,830 --> 00:53:55,398
This Is Choi-Seon's Best.

1427
00:53:56,967 --> 00:53:58,001
Like "the best," <i>choeseon</i>.

1428
00:53:58,068 --> 00:54:00,437
"Choi" Hyun-suk and Kim "Seon"-yeop.

1429
00:54:00,704 --> 00:54:03,173
KARINA, THIS IS CHOI-SEON'S BEST

1430
00:54:03,240 --> 00:54:05,275
First, for the lemonade,

1431
00:54:05,342 --> 00:54:07,444
since you seem to enjoy alcohol,

1432
00:54:07,510 --> 00:54:11,214
we're going to make lemonade for adults.

1433
00:54:11,281 --> 00:54:13,383
Also, you didn't have
lobster in your fridge,

1434
00:54:13,450 --> 00:54:16,886
but as the alchemists of culinary arts,
Team Chef Choi,

1435
00:54:16,953 --> 00:54:18,521
will make it for you.

1436
00:54:18,588 --> 00:54:20,624
You'll make lobster without lobster?

1437
00:54:20,690 --> 00:54:23,159
You can't make the lobster
like he did with the dinosaur.

1438
00:54:23,226 --> 00:54:26,129
You can't do that.

1439
00:54:26,196 --> 00:54:28,632
I guess he was inspired by me.

1440
00:54:28,698 --> 00:54:30,533
I put up with everything so far,

1441
00:54:30,600 --> 00:54:31,635
but that triggered me.

1442
00:54:31,701 --> 00:54:34,037
BECAUSE DINOSAURS ARE REALLY STRONG

1443
00:54:34,104 --> 00:54:37,274
Well, if he can make a dinosaur,
I'm sure you can make a lobster.

1444
00:54:38,208 --> 00:54:40,210
TEAM SON: SHRIMP, ZUCCHINI,
LEMON, CARROT, CELERY, ROMAINE

1445
00:54:40,277 --> 00:54:42,279
LANGOUSTINE SOUP, MILK,
POTATO, BEEF TENDERLOIN, AND MORE

1446
00:54:42,345 --> 00:54:45,715
Up against them are Chef Jong-won
and Chef Do-hee. What are you making?

1447
00:54:45,782 --> 00:54:49,152
We made an acrostic poem
with the syllables of "lemon."

1448
00:54:49,219 --> 00:54:52,355
An acrostic poem? That's creative.

1449
00:54:52,422 --> 00:54:55,425
You're quite the entertainer.
Let us start you off.

1450
00:54:55,492 --> 00:54:57,027
-Like a toast.
-I'll do it.

1451
00:54:57,093 --> 00:55:00,096
-Like a toast?
-All right. I'll start you off.

1452
00:55:00,163 --> 00:55:00,997
-Le.
-Le.

1453
00:55:01,064 --> 00:55:03,133
-Lemonade…
-And KARINA Are…

1454
00:55:03,199 --> 00:55:04,401
-Mon.
-Mon.

1455
00:55:04,467 --> 00:55:05,635
-…Monumental for Life.
-…Monumental for Life.

1456
00:55:05,935 --> 00:55:07,804
MELTED HER HEART

1457
00:55:08,104 --> 00:55:10,407
THAT WAS A NICE TOAST

1458
00:55:11,074 --> 00:55:12,709
They already get points for that name.

1459
00:55:12,776 --> 00:55:15,578
KARINA looks very pleased.

1460
00:55:15,645 --> 00:55:19,115
THE FRUIT OF THEIR PRACTICE

1461
00:55:19,182 --> 00:55:22,519
-So what is this dish?
-Oh, right.

1462
00:55:22,585 --> 00:55:24,554
-"Oh, right."
-We get the name.

1463
00:55:24,621 --> 00:55:26,456
-Monumental.
-What's the recipe?

1464
00:55:26,523 --> 00:55:29,459
First, we'll use zucchini
and shrimp to make mousse.

1465
00:55:29,526 --> 00:55:32,429
-Zucchini.
-We also found

1466
00:55:32,495 --> 00:55:35,198
canned langoustine stock,
so we'll make sauce with that.

1467
00:55:35,265 --> 00:55:37,167
Also, we'll make steak
topped with lemon pepper,

1468
00:55:37,233 --> 00:55:40,403
and lastly, lemon <i>hwachae</i>,
a total of three dishes.

1469
00:55:40,470 --> 00:55:41,805
-Chef Hyun-suk.
-Yes?

1470
00:55:41,871 --> 00:55:44,240
Shouldn't you also do an acrostic poem?

1471
00:55:44,307 --> 00:55:47,077
They already got some points.
KARINA melted a little.

1472
00:55:47,143 --> 00:55:48,978
If they worked together for 14 years,

1473
00:55:49,045 --> 00:55:51,247
-I'm sure they'll be better.
-Yes, of course.

1474
00:55:51,314 --> 00:55:52,982
HAVING FUN HEART

1475
00:55:53,049 --> 00:55:55,819
I mean, 14 years.
They'll do this much better

1476
00:55:55,885 --> 00:55:57,320
-than the team before.
-Of course.

1477
00:55:57,387 --> 00:56:00,757
-I wonder who'll do the first part.
-The first part?

1478
00:56:00,824 --> 00:56:02,459
-It's better to go first.
-Let's do it.

1479
00:56:02,525 --> 00:56:05,061
It's for Choi-Seon. One, two, three.

1480
00:56:05,128 --> 00:56:06,363
-Choi.
-Choi.

1481
00:56:06,930 --> 00:56:10,400
Choi Hyun-suk and other chefs' presence
is such an honor for me.

1482
00:56:10,467 --> 00:56:12,335
-That's nice.
-I'm not done yet.

1483
00:56:12,402 --> 00:56:14,037
Oh?

1484
00:56:14,104 --> 00:56:16,106
-And then?
-But Webtoon Artist Kim Poong.

1485
00:56:16,172 --> 00:56:18,775
-Suddenly I'm in it?
-"But Webtoon Artist Kim Poong."

1486
00:56:18,842 --> 00:56:20,310
That was it for the first syllable.

1487
00:56:20,377 --> 00:56:21,978
-Seon.
-Seon.

1488
00:56:22,045 --> 00:56:23,780
It's not,
"Seon-yeop will take you out," right?

1489
00:56:23,847 --> 00:56:24,881
-Seon.
-Seon.

1490
00:56:27,050 --> 00:56:28,585
It's going to be really funny.

1491
00:56:28,651 --> 00:56:29,919
-It'll be funny.
-Seon.

1492
00:56:29,986 --> 00:56:31,755
I'm so excited.

1493
00:56:32,322 --> 00:56:33,356
Lights down, please.

1494
00:56:34,290 --> 00:56:36,826
Let's start cooking!

1495
00:56:36,893 --> 00:56:39,229
-He couldn't finish it.
-What a shame.

1496
00:56:39,295 --> 00:56:41,598
Do, "Seon-yeop will teach you a lesson."

1497
00:56:41,664 --> 00:56:43,733
-Seon.
-Seon-yeop will teach you a lesson.

1498
00:56:43,967 --> 00:56:45,668
STILL PLEASED

1499
00:56:45,735 --> 00:56:47,370
WAS THAT TRULY YOUR BEST?

1500
00:56:47,437 --> 00:56:50,240
Now, a round just for KARINA.

1501
00:56:50,306 --> 00:56:52,008
Lights down, please.

1502
00:56:52,075 --> 00:56:53,076
Timer, please.

1503
00:56:53,143 --> 00:56:54,744
ONLY ONE TEAM WILL RISE ABOVE!

1504
00:56:54,811 --> 00:56:59,382
A never-before-seen cook-off,
the first time in the show's history.

1505
00:56:59,449 --> 00:57:01,117
-Wow.
-Wow.

1506
00:57:01,184 --> 00:57:03,620
They'll play as a team
with their right-hand men.

1507
00:57:03,686 --> 00:57:08,224
Hyun-suk and Seon-yeop will prove
the 14 years they spent together.

1508
00:57:08,291 --> 00:57:12,028
The chefs who are passionate
about both cooking and team dinners,

1509
00:57:12,095 --> 00:57:15,031
Jong-won and Do-hee are up against them.

1510
00:57:15,098 --> 00:57:18,802
The first team round
with the chefs' right-hand men!

1511
00:57:18,868 --> 00:57:20,303
Which team will be the winner?

1512
00:57:20,370 --> 00:57:23,473
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1513
00:57:23,540 --> 00:57:25,041
WOW!

1514
00:57:25,742 --> 00:57:28,011
TEAM SON MOVES FAST!

1515
00:57:28,511 --> 00:57:29,979
Let's see what they do.

1516
00:57:30,313 --> 00:57:31,881
1. MAKE SYRUP BY BOILING WATER AND SUGAR

1517
00:57:31,948 --> 00:57:34,350
-Jong-won and Do-hee divided the tasks.
-Yes.

1518
00:57:34,417 --> 00:57:38,321
Their timetable is planned out
in 30-second intervals.

1519
00:57:38,688 --> 00:57:42,592
2. MAKE SHRIMP MOUSSE BY GRINDING
SHRIMP, STARCH WATER, AND EGG WHITES

1520
00:57:43,793 --> 00:57:45,795
Jong-won chops the zucchini.

1521
00:57:45,862 --> 00:57:47,330
Their plans

1522
00:57:47,397 --> 00:57:49,566
are in 30-second intervals.

1523
00:57:51,568 --> 00:57:54,704
-I don't know who to look at.
-Right? I'm busy looking at everyone.

1524
00:57:54,771 --> 00:57:57,640
Seon-yeop preps shrimp.

1525
00:57:57,707 --> 00:57:59,642
-Hyun-suk is on eggs.
-Eggs.

1526
00:57:59,709 --> 00:58:01,744
He's doing the easier job.

1527
00:58:03,012 --> 00:58:05,448
Chef Seon-yeop, give me an order
if you need me to do something.

1528
00:58:05,515 --> 00:58:08,017
I guess Seon-yeop is the main chef.

1529
00:58:08,084 --> 00:58:09,619
-Give me the mousse right away.
-Yes, sir.

1530
00:58:10,753 --> 00:58:12,422
-Mousse and diced veggies.
-Yes, sir.

1531
00:58:12,489 --> 00:58:13,857
It's been 30 seconds!

1532
00:58:14,090 --> 00:58:14,991
ALREADY?

1533
00:58:15,058 --> 00:58:17,360
-Already? Will they move on?
-It's already been 30 seconds.

1534
00:58:18,261 --> 00:58:20,864
They'll have to move on every 30 seconds.

1535
00:58:21,364 --> 00:58:23,867
3. MIX SHRIMP MOUSSE
WITH LEMON ZEST AND STARCH

1536
00:58:24,467 --> 00:58:27,537
4. SLICE ZUCCHINI THINLY

1537
00:58:27,604 --> 00:58:28,972
-Spin that.
-Yes, Chef.

1538
00:58:29,038 --> 00:58:31,541
-Another 30.
-That was another 30 seconds.

1539
00:58:31,608 --> 00:58:35,378
-It's been a minute already.
-Another 30 seconds!

1540
00:58:35,445 --> 00:58:37,514
-They're still on the first task.
-The first task.

1541
00:58:37,580 --> 00:58:39,916
-It's been a minute.
-They've been doing that for a minute.

1542
00:58:40,250 --> 00:58:41,684
THE GOOD VIBES DOPPELGÄNGERS

1543
00:58:42,018 --> 00:58:45,388
-Spin that.
-Yes, Chef.

1544
00:58:45,955 --> 00:58:46,890
SHRIMP MOUSSE, FINISHED

1545
00:58:46,956 --> 00:58:48,958
-Dice the veggies and grind the pepper.
-Yes, Chef.

1546
00:58:49,025 --> 00:58:50,960
GIVING ACCURATE ORDERS!

1547
00:58:51,027 --> 00:58:52,228
One, two, three.

1548
00:58:54,364 --> 00:58:55,832
GIVING ACCURATE ORDERS?!

1549
00:58:55,899 --> 00:58:57,901
-They just salted the food!
-I knew it.

1550
00:58:57,967 --> 00:58:59,936
<i>Salty and sweet</i>

1551
00:59:00,003 --> 00:59:03,172
-They prepared something too.
-The salt cross.

1552
00:59:03,239 --> 00:59:06,342
KARINA asked you
to salt her food many times.

1553
00:59:07,043 --> 00:59:11,080
-Is Chef Hyun-suk doing the busywork?
-I think so.

1554
00:59:11,147 --> 00:59:14,117
Seon-yeop seems to be doing the main job.

1555
00:59:14,183 --> 00:59:16,719
They said they'd make lobster,

1556
00:59:16,786 --> 00:59:19,889
so I have a feeling
they'll do it with shrimp.

1557
00:59:19,956 --> 00:59:22,425
Seon-yeop is trying to create
something from nothing!

1558
00:59:22,492 --> 00:59:25,895
-By stacking layers of shrimp?
-Yes, I think that's it.

1559
00:59:25,962 --> 00:59:26,896
WOW

1560
00:59:26,963 --> 00:59:28,698
Just make one if you have no time.

1561
00:59:28,765 --> 00:59:30,133
-I'll make just two.
-Hmm?

1562
00:59:30,199 --> 00:59:31,434
-I'll make just two.
-Hmm?

1563
00:59:31,501 --> 00:59:33,169
I'll make two.

1564
00:59:33,236 --> 00:59:35,872
Jong-won and Do-hee
are working on vegetables.

1565
00:59:35,939 --> 00:59:37,907
Carrot and zucchini.

1566
00:59:37,974 --> 00:59:41,578
Do-hee is quite fast too.

1567
00:59:41,644 --> 00:59:46,082
DICE ONION, CARROT, CELERY

1568
00:59:46,649 --> 00:59:48,418
-Are you done dicing?
-Sorry?

1569
00:59:48,484 --> 00:59:51,087
-How much longer do you need?
-Thirty seconds, Chef.

1570
00:59:51,154 --> 00:59:54,824
ONLY 30 SECONDS
TO DICE ONION, CARROT, AND CELERY?

1571
00:59:55,391 --> 00:59:57,193
-I'm done dicing, Chef.
-Okay.

1572
00:59:57,260 --> 00:59:58,328
CARROT, ONION, CELERY
ONION

1573
00:59:58,394 --> 01:00:00,697
It's hard to tell what's going on
with two chefs on each side.

1574
01:00:00,763 --> 01:00:02,065
Chef Seon-yeop.

1575
01:00:02,131 --> 01:00:04,200
-Don't be nervous. We got this.
-Yes.

1576
01:00:04,267 --> 01:00:07,070
Chef Seon-yeop, trust me.

1577
01:00:07,136 --> 01:00:09,439
Hyun-suk keeps talking to Seon-yeop.

1578
01:00:09,505 --> 01:00:10,673
They'll fill shrimp with shrimp.

1579
01:00:10,740 --> 01:00:12,008
-Minced shrimp.
-What's that?

1580
01:00:12,075 --> 01:00:13,343
-What's that?
-What is it, Hyun-suk?

1581
01:00:13,409 --> 01:00:15,144
Why are you sifting the shrimp?

1582
01:00:15,211 --> 01:00:19,682
He'll sift it, put it on the shrimp,
and make lobster shapes.

1583
01:00:19,749 --> 01:00:22,719
-Wow.
-So he needs that to make the lobster.

1584
01:00:22,785 --> 01:00:25,888
We're here to show our best performance,

1585
01:00:25,955 --> 01:00:29,626
which means fine dining-like
soft texture is important.

1586
01:00:29,692 --> 01:00:32,962
The texture, the presentation,
the taste, and the flavor.

1587
01:00:33,029 --> 01:00:34,364
We won't miss anything.

1588
01:00:34,430 --> 01:00:36,733
That's him.

1589
01:00:37,033 --> 01:00:40,003
3. SIFT THE SHRIMP PASTE

1590
01:00:40,069 --> 01:00:41,838
Aren't taste and flavor the same thing?

1591
01:00:42,405 --> 01:00:43,406
The savoriness.

1592
01:00:43,973 --> 01:00:46,075
-The savoriness.
-Right.

1593
01:00:46,142 --> 01:00:49,779
-Chef Jong-won's making something.
-He's making something

1594
01:00:49,846 --> 01:00:52,482
-with tweezers.
-A zucchini flower filled with shrimp.

1595
01:00:52,548 --> 01:00:54,651
-Zucchini flower?
-Whoa.

1596
01:00:54,717 --> 01:00:56,085
Shrimp and zucchini flower?

1597
01:00:56,152 --> 01:00:57,620
That's a dinosaur eye.

1598
01:00:58,321 --> 01:01:00,223
-Is it a dinosaur?
-A dinosaur eye, right?

1599
01:01:00,289 --> 01:01:01,424
He must've been inspired.

1600
01:01:01,991 --> 01:01:03,860
-He inspires me often.
-How delicate.

1601
01:01:03,926 --> 01:01:06,029
-He's very detailed.
-Like that?

1602
01:01:06,095 --> 01:01:09,966
-No one seems confused.
-Both teams have great teamwork.

1603
01:01:10,033 --> 01:01:12,935
-Wow. How do they do that?
-The soup base.

1604
01:01:13,002 --> 01:01:17,240
He'll probably do his best to create
lobster's flavor and texture.

1605
01:01:17,306 --> 01:01:18,174
When she tries it,

1606
01:01:18,241 --> 01:01:20,143
-she can't go, "It's a shrimp."
-She shouldn't.

1607
01:01:20,209 --> 01:01:21,411
-She can't.
-That's not good.

1608
01:01:21,477 --> 01:01:24,247
-You're right.
-It should look similar too.

1609
01:01:24,313 --> 01:01:26,082
Yes, of course.

1610
01:01:26,149 --> 01:01:30,253
She didn't have a lobster,

1611
01:01:30,319 --> 01:01:32,121
so can they really make it?

1612
01:01:32,188 --> 01:01:33,756
-Is this enough, Chef?
-What?

1613
01:01:34,490 --> 01:01:36,025
I TRUST YOU!

1614
01:01:36,459 --> 01:01:38,695
FINISHING UP BY ROLLING IT IN COOKING FOIL

1615
01:01:39,462 --> 01:01:40,997
Huh?

1616
01:01:41,064 --> 01:01:42,065
<i>Gomtang ramyeon</i>?

1617
01:01:42,131 --> 01:01:45,234
-<i>Ramyeon </i>soup base.
-MSG! <i>Ramyeon </i>soup base.

1618
01:01:45,301 --> 01:01:47,704
-I love it.
-He said he'd make gravy with that.

1619
01:01:47,770 --> 01:01:49,605
It's made with beef bones anyway,

1620
01:01:49,672 --> 01:01:51,841
just like how <i>gomtang</i>
is made with beef bone broth.

1621
01:01:51,908 --> 01:01:56,479
-But gravy has to be darker in color.
-He'll make it happen.

1622
01:01:56,546 --> 01:02:00,149
-It's gomtang <i>ramyeon</i> soup base.
-Will he boil the soup base?

1623
01:02:00,216 --> 01:02:02,719
-Let's boil down wine.
-He's boiling it.

1624
01:02:02,985 --> 01:02:05,588
GOMTANG RAMYEON SOUP,
RED WINE, SIMPLE SYRUP

1625
01:02:05,888 --> 01:02:07,824
JONG-WON IS DONE MAKING SYRUP

1626
01:02:08,825 --> 01:02:10,593
-Simple syrup.
-Yes, Chef.

1627
01:02:10,660 --> 01:02:12,095
I'll crush the pepper.

1628
01:02:14,063 --> 01:02:17,600
We'll crush the pepper,
then coat the steak before grilling it.

1629
01:02:17,667 --> 01:02:19,535
Like pepper sauce?

1630
01:02:19,936 --> 01:02:21,571
AH-HA!

1631
01:02:21,637 --> 01:02:23,039
Why can't you use a pepper grinder?

1632
01:02:23,106 --> 01:02:25,942
The pieces need to be bigger
to make the pepper sauce.

1633
01:02:26,008 --> 01:02:27,977
-I see.
-Crushing.

1634
01:02:28,044 --> 01:02:29,245
He's not using a grinder.

1635
01:02:29,312 --> 01:02:32,014
-The aroma is stronger that way too.
-I see.

1636
01:02:32,415 --> 01:02:34,250
OPENS THE STEAMER

1637
01:02:34,317 --> 01:02:35,384
It's going in.

1638
01:02:36,986 --> 01:02:39,222
Zucchini enters the steamer.

1639
01:02:39,288 --> 01:02:41,624
-Zucchini enters the steamer.
-Okay.

1640
01:02:41,824 --> 01:02:42,658
COME ON!

1641
01:02:42,925 --> 01:02:45,428
CARROT & ONION & CELERY

1642
01:02:45,795 --> 01:02:47,163
Ten minutes.

1643
01:02:48,097 --> 01:02:48,931
Bisque.

1644
01:02:48,998 --> 01:02:51,434
-Langoustine bisque.
-Langoustine bisque.

1645
01:02:51,501 --> 01:02:53,936
It's been five minutes,
Jong-won and Do-hee.

1646
01:02:54,003 --> 01:02:56,372
-It's gravy versus bisque.
-Yes, gravy versus bisque.

1647
01:02:56,439 --> 01:02:57,306
What was that?

1648
01:02:59,542 --> 01:03:00,877
THE SALT YOU ORDERED!

1649
01:03:01,244 --> 01:03:04,447
WAS THIS WHAT HE MEANT
BY CLEANING UP AFTER HIS MESS?

1650
01:03:05,715 --> 01:03:07,550
They came prepared.

1651
01:03:08,417 --> 01:03:10,119
They're just… Gosh.

1652
01:03:10,186 --> 01:03:11,954
They prepared that to win aespa's favor.

1653
01:03:12,021 --> 01:03:15,758
Hyun-suk and Seon-yeop
don't forget to put on a performance.

1654
01:03:15,825 --> 01:03:18,661
I think they just won
a few points from her.

1655
01:03:18,728 --> 01:03:19,762
8. GRILL RIB EYE IN A PAN

1656
01:03:20,096 --> 01:03:23,499
TEAM SON STARTS ON THEIR SECOND DISH

1657
01:03:23,566 --> 01:03:25,768
-How long will <i>pommes soufflées</i> take?
-<i>Pommes soufflées</i>?

1658
01:03:25,835 --> 01:03:26,969
Just one minute, Chef.

1659
01:03:27,036 --> 01:03:30,139
-He's patting the potato slices dry.
-Will he fry those?

1660
01:03:30,206 --> 01:03:31,240
Will he fry those?

1661
01:03:31,307 --> 01:03:32,842
Chef Do-hee, will you fry those?

1662
01:03:32,909 --> 01:03:33,910
Yes, Chef.

1663
01:03:33,976 --> 01:03:36,045
He just said, "Yes, Chef."

1664
01:03:36,112 --> 01:03:37,280
-Did he call me a chef?
-He did.

1665
01:03:38,281 --> 01:03:39,382
Thank you, Chef!

1666
01:03:40,016 --> 01:03:41,684
Chef Do-hee.

1667
01:03:41,751 --> 01:03:43,486
No one has ever called me that before.

1668
01:03:43,553 --> 01:03:45,121
Whenever Jong-won said something,

1669
01:03:45,188 --> 01:03:48,424
Do-hee kept answering, "Yes, Chef,"
in a soft voice.

1670
01:03:48,491 --> 01:03:51,727
It's amazing that a chef
who came for the first time can respond.

1671
01:03:51,794 --> 01:03:54,297
-It means he can hear you.
-It does.

1672
01:03:54,363 --> 01:03:57,700
-It also means he can hear Chef Jong-won.
-That's true.

1673
01:03:57,767 --> 01:03:59,235
Better than me. I couldn't respond.

1674
01:03:59,302 --> 01:04:02,238
-He'll make them puffy.
-The potato garnish he's making

1675
01:04:02,305 --> 01:04:04,974
will look like puffy balloons.

1676
01:04:05,041 --> 01:04:07,610
-By sticking them together?
-They'll puff up.

1677
01:04:07,677 --> 01:04:08,544
It's tough to make.

1678
01:04:09,111 --> 01:04:10,379
Careful. It's the syrup.

1679
01:04:10,780 --> 01:04:12,148
SHRIMP ROLL

1680
01:04:12,949 --> 01:04:14,584
What did you put in, Seon-yeop?

1681
01:04:14,650 --> 01:04:15,885
The shrimp cake.

1682
01:04:15,952 --> 01:04:17,053
He can respond too.

1683
01:04:17,119 --> 01:04:18,621
-He can.
-Egg yolks.

1684
01:04:18,688 --> 01:04:22,024
They're listening to their head chefs.

1685
01:04:22,091 --> 01:04:23,259
Did you add salt?

1686
01:04:23,326 --> 01:04:24,527
No.

1687
01:04:24,861 --> 01:04:25,761
COARSELY GROUND BLACK PEPPER

1688
01:04:26,062 --> 01:04:28,497
SALT, LEMON ZEST

1689
01:04:29,498 --> 01:04:33,102
He's tying the tenderloin
and turning it into a medal

1690
01:04:33,169 --> 01:04:35,605
-to keep the shape together.
-There's the steak.

1691
01:04:35,671 --> 01:04:38,941
He's working on the steak.

1692
01:04:39,542 --> 01:04:41,544
Hyun-suk is grilling meat too.

1693
01:04:42,011 --> 01:04:43,913
PERFECT CRUST!

1694
01:04:43,980 --> 01:04:46,349
-That's nicely done.
-It looks delicious.

1695
01:04:46,415 --> 01:04:48,818
Jong-won's steak looks a bit prettier.

1696
01:04:48,885 --> 01:04:51,520
He's using tenderloin,
and I think that's strip loin.

1697
01:04:51,587 --> 01:04:53,356
He's about to deep-fry them.

1698
01:04:53,422 --> 01:04:54,724
-It's 183°C. Go.
-He's deep-frying.

1699
01:04:54,790 --> 01:04:56,592
I hope those turn out nicely.

1700
01:04:56,659 --> 01:04:58,294
Those are hard to make.

1701
01:04:58,361 --> 01:05:00,062
-What's hard?
-Deep-frying those.

1702
01:05:00,129 --> 01:05:00,963
It's not hard.

1703
01:05:01,030 --> 01:05:04,333
-They have to get puffy.
-They have to puff up.

1704
01:05:04,400 --> 01:05:05,501
-The potatoes?
-Yes.

1705
01:05:05,568 --> 01:05:06,602
It's tough to do.

1706
01:05:06,669 --> 01:05:08,337
-Do-hee is up for a tough task.
-Careful.

1707
01:05:08,404 --> 01:05:09,505
Like tiny balloons.

1708
01:05:09,572 --> 01:05:11,474
-Precisely.
-But with potatoes.

1709
01:05:11,540 --> 01:05:14,477
-They're getting puffy.
-It's their steak garnish.

1710
01:05:14,543 --> 01:05:17,580
Do-hee shows off his technique!

1711
01:05:17,647 --> 01:05:19,715
He needs to pour oil on top
to help them get puffier.

1712
01:05:19,782 --> 01:05:22,385
He made the potato pieces puff up.

1713
01:05:22,451 --> 01:05:23,352
Do-hee, the bread is in.

1714
01:05:23,419 --> 01:05:24,654
-I haven't done it.
-I did.

1715
01:05:24,720 --> 01:05:25,655
<i>Oui.</i>

1716
01:05:25,721 --> 01:05:26,689
"<i>Oui</i>, Chef."

1717
01:05:26,756 --> 01:05:28,591
-<i>Oui?</i>
-<i>Oui</i>, Chef. It's yes in French.

1718
01:05:28,658 --> 01:05:29,959
It means yes?

1719
01:05:30,026 --> 01:05:32,995
-That's tough to do.
-Team Jong-won shows a good technique.

1720
01:05:33,062 --> 01:05:34,163
-They look good.
-I knew it.

1721
01:05:34,230 --> 01:05:37,433
Hyun-suk and Seon-yeop
are each doing their own thing.

1722
01:05:38,567 --> 01:05:41,604
QUIETLY PREPARING THE WATERMELON GARNISH

1723
01:05:41,671 --> 01:05:44,573
9. CHOP WATERMELON INTO A BAR
AND GRILL IN BUTTER

1724
01:05:44,640 --> 01:05:46,709
It smells like a fine dining restaurant.

1725
01:05:46,976 --> 01:05:47,944
CHUCKLING

1726
01:05:48,010 --> 01:05:49,145
It smells like it.

1727
01:05:49,211 --> 01:05:51,547
May I recite another acrostic poem?

1728
01:05:51,614 --> 01:05:54,650
-An acrostic poem? With what?
-All of a sudden?

1729
01:05:54,717 --> 01:05:55,818
Please start me off.

1730
01:05:55,885 --> 01:05:57,019
What's the word?

1731
01:05:57,086 --> 01:05:58,421
-Lemon?
-Lemon, please.

1732
01:05:58,487 --> 01:05:59,689
SPRINKLING SALT!

1733
01:06:00,122 --> 01:06:01,524
WHAT'S THIS NOW?

1734
01:06:01,590 --> 01:06:03,192
Salt!

1735
01:06:03,259 --> 01:06:04,493
CUTTING OFF THE ACROSTIC POEM!

1736
01:06:04,560 --> 01:06:07,496
-He was about to do the poem!
-He cuts the poem off!

1737
01:06:07,763 --> 01:06:08,998
THIS IS HILARIOUS

1738
01:06:09,065 --> 01:06:10,433
He didn't want to lose attention.

1739
01:06:10,499 --> 01:06:11,834
BASTING WITH BUTTER

1740
01:06:11,901 --> 01:06:13,202
13. COOK THE TENDERLOIN IN THE OVEN

1741
01:06:13,269 --> 01:06:15,838
I thought Sung-jun would be too talkative,

1742
01:06:15,905 --> 01:06:18,174
-so I had prepared it for him.
-What is it?

1743
01:06:18,240 --> 01:06:19,976
-One, two, three. Le!
-Le!

1744
01:06:20,042 --> 01:06:21,877
Lemon, hold it.

1745
01:06:21,944 --> 01:06:23,012
-One, two, three. Mon!
-Mon!

1746
01:06:23,079 --> 01:06:24,080
Monster.

1747
01:06:24,647 --> 01:06:26,248
CAUGHT OFF GUARD

1748
01:06:26,315 --> 01:06:27,817
He had that prepared.

1749
01:06:27,883 --> 01:06:28,851
That was random.

1750
01:06:28,918 --> 01:06:30,987
-That was so random.
-So random.

1751
01:06:31,053 --> 01:06:34,657
-That was awful.
-Sung-jun suddenly got attacked.

1752
01:06:34,724 --> 01:06:36,592
He was caught off guard.

1753
01:06:36,659 --> 01:06:39,662
I prepared it,
so I wanted to use it on anyone.

1754
01:06:39,729 --> 01:06:42,932
-With that lemon.
-He's just a monster with a lemon.

1755
01:06:42,999 --> 01:06:45,501
It's hard to stop late bloomers.

1756
01:06:45,568 --> 01:06:47,737
"Lemon, hold this. You monster."

1757
01:06:47,803 --> 01:06:49,205
Sorry, Sung-jun.

1758
01:06:49,271 --> 01:06:51,073
Jong-won and Do-hee couple…

1759
01:06:51,140 --> 01:06:52,575
"Couple"?

1760
01:06:52,641 --> 01:06:55,444
Not only are they good at cooking,
but they're funny too.

1761
01:06:55,511 --> 01:06:58,614
They're funny and have good teamwork too.

1762
01:06:58,681 --> 01:06:59,915
Six minutes left!

1763
01:07:00,783 --> 01:07:02,351
-There's no time.
-Six minutes.

1764
01:07:02,418 --> 01:07:03,753
You should start wrapping up.

1765
01:07:03,819 --> 01:07:06,455
-Did they get puffy? Wow.
-Two pieces.

1766
01:07:06,522 --> 01:07:07,690
They became puffy.

1767
01:07:09,225 --> 01:07:10,559
-Did you add salt?
-I didn't.

1768
01:07:10,626 --> 01:07:11,994
-Take this.
-Okay.

1769
01:07:12,061 --> 01:07:13,963
COVERING

1770
01:07:15,431 --> 01:07:18,000
SEON-YEOP STRAINS MELTED BUTTER

1771
01:07:18,300 --> 01:07:19,969
COOL DOWN, PLEASE

1772
01:07:20,036 --> 01:07:22,905
Is Chef Seon-yeop

1773
01:07:22,972 --> 01:07:24,206
making mayonnaise?

1774
01:07:24,273 --> 01:07:26,275
-Is that mayo?
-I'm making hollandaise.

1775
01:07:26,342 --> 01:07:29,578
-Hollandaise?
-Hollandaise.

1776
01:07:29,645 --> 01:07:31,414
These dishes
all require lots of techniques.

1777
01:07:31,480 --> 01:07:34,950
-We have no idea what's going on now.
-Right?

1778
01:07:36,185 --> 01:07:37,286
ALL RIGHT

1779
01:07:37,486 --> 01:07:39,688
PUTTING BUTTER INTO A SYRINGE

1780
01:07:39,755 --> 01:07:40,689
LOCKED IN

1781
01:07:42,091 --> 01:07:44,493
WHAT'S THIS TOOL FOR?

1782
01:07:46,796 --> 01:07:50,266
-What's that? What's he doing?
-What is that, Do-hee?

1783
01:07:50,332 --> 01:07:52,268
-Is that butter?
-Butter.

1784
01:07:52,334 --> 01:07:54,203
-You can do that?
-He squeezed butter out.

1785
01:07:54,470 --> 01:07:55,438
WOW…

1786
01:07:55,504 --> 01:07:57,440
-How cool.
-It looks like ribbon.

1787
01:07:57,506 --> 01:07:59,341
I want that thing too.

1788
01:07:59,408 --> 01:08:01,477
-It's like knife-cut noodles.
-Is that a flower?

1789
01:08:04,580 --> 01:08:08,350
Do-hee makes that
to capture the lady's heart.

1790
01:08:08,417 --> 01:08:10,052
That's so pretty.

1791
01:08:10,119 --> 01:08:11,520
Are you intrigued, KARINA?

1792
01:08:11,587 --> 01:08:12,988
SO INTRIGUED

1793
01:08:13,055 --> 01:08:15,624
-How pretty.
-That's so pretty.

1794
01:08:15,691 --> 01:08:16,659
15. MAKE FLOWER BUTTER

1795
01:08:16,725 --> 01:08:18,794
-I want that thing too.
-That's nice.

1796
01:08:18,861 --> 01:08:20,096
Five minutes left.

1797
01:08:20,162 --> 01:08:22,765
Jung-hwan, please go to the kitchen.

1798
01:08:22,832 --> 01:08:23,999
It's too crowded over there.

1799
01:08:24,066 --> 01:08:27,903
Which team has better teamwork?

1800
01:08:27,970 --> 01:08:29,171
It's crowded.

1801
01:08:29,238 --> 01:08:31,540
-It's so crowded over here.
-Four minutes left.

1802
01:08:31,607 --> 01:08:33,642
Chef Do-hee squeezes a lemon.

1803
01:08:33,709 --> 01:08:35,077
16. MIX SYRUP AND LEMON JUICE

1804
01:08:35,144 --> 01:08:36,078
Hey, Chef Jong-won.

1805
01:08:36,745 --> 01:08:37,947
ZUCCHINI SHRIMP MOUSSE, FINISHED

1806
01:08:38,013 --> 01:08:39,515
There are so many sauces.

1807
01:08:40,549 --> 01:08:41,517
There are, right?

1808
01:08:41,584 --> 01:08:43,385
Yes, too many. It's so complicated here.

1809
01:08:43,886 --> 01:08:46,288
-That thing.
-He's cooking watermelon.

1810
01:08:46,355 --> 01:08:47,623
-Is that watermelon?
-Yes.

1811
01:08:47,690 --> 01:08:50,092
-Watermelon?
-He's still cooking the steak.

1812
01:08:50,993 --> 01:08:53,329
-He adds balsamic vinegar.
-Balsamic vinegar.

1813
01:08:53,395 --> 01:08:54,697
Watermelon and balsamic.

1814
01:08:54,763 --> 01:08:57,133
-They work well as a team.
-Jung-hwan!

1815
01:08:57,199 --> 01:08:59,969
Can you try Seon-yeop's sauce?

1816
01:09:00,035 --> 01:09:01,504
It's hollandaise.

1817
01:09:01,570 --> 01:09:04,073
-Okay!
-Let me try this.

1818
01:09:04,140 --> 01:09:05,241
Jung-hwan!

1819
01:09:05,307 --> 01:09:06,809
Is that Choi-Seon's best?

1820
01:09:06,876 --> 01:09:08,344
Is that Choi-Seon's best?

1821
01:09:08,410 --> 01:09:10,779
-This is the best!
-Is it really?

1822
01:09:11,113 --> 01:09:12,114
EXCITED

1823
01:09:12,181 --> 01:09:14,350
-It's great.
-Nothing wrong with it?

1824
01:09:14,416 --> 01:09:16,819
It's salty, and full of umami.

1825
01:09:17,119 --> 01:09:18,087
SCALLIONS

1826
01:09:18,154 --> 01:09:21,490
-They kept throwing salt in.
-They did.

1827
01:09:21,557 --> 01:09:23,659
-Wait, what about the lobster?
-The steamer.

1828
01:09:23,726 --> 01:09:25,361
It's in the steamer?

1829
01:09:25,427 --> 01:09:26,362
I hope it turns out well.

1830
01:09:26,428 --> 01:09:28,063
It tasted very nice.

1831
01:09:28,130 --> 01:09:29,632
There's a feel to it.

1832
01:09:29,698 --> 01:09:31,133
It must be salty and delicious.

1833
01:09:31,200 --> 01:09:32,601
Seon-yeop's adding butter now.

1834
01:09:33,369 --> 01:09:35,404
I'll hollow out the lemon.

1835
01:09:35,471 --> 01:09:37,706
-Four minutes, Do-hee.
-Yes, Chef.

1836
01:09:37,773 --> 01:09:39,241
-And the Parisian cut.
-<i>Oui.</i>

1837
01:09:39,975 --> 01:09:41,777
HOLLOWING OUT THE LEMON!

1838
01:09:41,844 --> 01:09:42,912
MELON & MANGO

1839
01:09:42,978 --> 01:09:44,246
-Cut these out.
-<i>Oui.</i>

1840
01:09:44,313 --> 01:09:46,682
I'll finish these and crush ice.

1841
01:09:46,749 --> 01:09:47,783
Melon?

1842
01:09:47,850 --> 01:09:53,989
PARISIAN CUT: FINE DINING TECHNIQUE
TO SCOOP FRUITS AND VEGGIES INTO SPHERES

1843
01:09:55,291 --> 01:09:56,125
MISSION COMPLETE!

1844
01:09:56,192 --> 01:09:57,526
I'll crush the ice, Chef.

1845
01:09:57,593 --> 01:09:59,895
Crush the ice first

1846
01:10:00,462 --> 01:10:02,598
-and fill the plate.
-<i>Oui.</i>

1847
01:10:02,665 --> 01:10:04,667
Three minutes left!

1848
01:10:04,733 --> 01:10:06,435
He crushed some ice too.

1849
01:10:06,502 --> 01:10:09,171
Jong-won and Do-hee
are only prepping the ingredients.

1850
01:10:09,238 --> 01:10:11,207
17. CRUSH ICE, FILL A CUP,
TOP IT WITH HOLLOWED-OUT LEMON

1851
01:10:11,273 --> 01:10:12,675
The bisque sauce.

1852
01:10:12,741 --> 01:10:14,210
The bisque sauce. This one?

1853
01:10:14,276 --> 01:10:15,978
-It's this one.
-They look the same.

1854
01:10:16,045 --> 01:10:17,146
-The farther one?
-It's the same.

1855
01:10:17,213 --> 01:10:18,514
-It's the same.
-Try one.

1856
01:10:18,581 --> 01:10:20,249
They're different.

1857
01:10:20,316 --> 01:10:21,383
-Try it, Jung-hwan.
-Oh, no.

1858
01:10:21,450 --> 01:10:23,886
-They're the same. Which one should I try?
-They're different.

1859
01:10:23,953 --> 01:10:25,154
-They're different?
-Try both.

1860
01:10:25,221 --> 01:10:26,822
-Both?
-They're very different.

1861
01:10:26,889 --> 01:10:27,823
The farther one first, then.

1862
01:10:27,890 --> 01:10:29,458
The sauce from the farther pot.

1863
01:10:29,525 --> 01:10:33,362
Will KARINA find the sauce monumental?

1864
01:10:33,429 --> 01:10:34,530
ANTICIPATION

1865
01:10:35,130 --> 01:10:37,733
-It's monumental!
-Whoa!

1866
01:10:37,800 --> 01:10:40,035
-The sauce's monumental.
-Monumental.

1867
01:10:40,102 --> 01:10:41,403
It's so good.

1868
01:10:41,470 --> 01:10:42,638
Was that the bisque?

1869
01:10:42,705 --> 01:10:45,774
It has that
classic instant cream soup flavor.

1870
01:10:45,841 --> 01:10:47,109
WAVES

1871
01:10:47,176 --> 01:10:49,645
"I need your attention."

1872
01:10:49,912 --> 01:10:52,248
HYUN-SUK'S MOTTO:
A DISH LIKE A COMEDY FILM!

1873
01:10:52,548 --> 01:10:54,450
WHAT'S HE SHAKING EXCITEDLY?

1874
01:10:54,683 --> 01:10:56,452
KARINA'S FAVORITE, RUBY KIWI

1875
01:10:57,052 --> 01:10:59,688
AND THE MAIN INGREDIENT, LEMON!

1876
01:11:00,055 --> 01:11:04,727
14. SHAKE RUBY KIWI, LEMON JUICE,
AND ICE IN A COCKTAIL SHAKER

1877
01:11:05,394 --> 01:11:07,463
It has that
classic instant cream soup flavor.

1878
01:11:07,529 --> 01:11:08,897
WAVES

1879
01:11:08,964 --> 01:11:10,266
"I need your attention."

1880
01:11:10,332 --> 01:11:11,500
-Mister.
-Hyun-suk!

1881
01:11:11,567 --> 01:11:13,469
He doesn't forget
to create an entertaining moment.

1882
01:11:14,903 --> 01:11:16,705
I'm about to faint.

1883
01:11:16,772 --> 01:11:17,906
-Lemonade.
-So funny.

1884
01:11:17,973 --> 01:11:21,443
He's serious about these performances.

1885
01:11:21,510 --> 01:11:23,579
-He kept doing the wave.
-Jung-hwan!

1886
01:11:23,646 --> 01:11:25,047
I think that's the lobster.

1887
01:11:25,114 --> 01:11:26,849
Is this lobster?

1888
01:11:26,915 --> 01:11:27,916
Try this piece.

1889
01:11:28,350 --> 01:11:29,318
FILMING FIRST

1890
01:11:29,385 --> 01:11:31,820
How did it turn out like lobster?

1891
01:11:31,887 --> 01:11:33,622
Hey, it's lobster!

1892
01:11:33,689 --> 01:11:35,257
It's lobster!

1893
01:11:35,324 --> 01:11:37,092
Hey, it looks like one.

1894
01:11:38,127 --> 01:11:40,462
CHOI-SEON'S MAGIC!

1895
01:11:40,529 --> 01:11:41,964
-It's lobster.
-She didn't have it.

1896
01:11:42,031 --> 01:11:43,332
LOOKS LIKE PLUMP LOBSTER MEAT

1897
01:11:43,399 --> 01:11:45,134
Let me have a taste.

1898
01:11:45,200 --> 01:11:47,169
-The tip.
-Is it like lobster?

1899
01:11:48,103 --> 01:11:50,539
-Does it taste like lobster?
-Does it?

1900
01:11:50,606 --> 01:11:52,608
-This is insane. It's lobster.
-It is?

1901
01:11:52,675 --> 01:11:53,676
-It's lobster!
-It is?

1902
01:11:54,176 --> 01:11:56,278
THE MAGIC FOOLED BOTH HIS EYES AND PALATE

1903
01:11:56,345 --> 01:11:58,380
It has the texture of a "matster,"
I mean, lobster too.

1904
01:11:58,647 --> 01:11:59,848
DID YOU JUST CALL ME?

1905
01:11:59,915 --> 01:12:01,550
-This is amazing.
-"Matster."

1906
01:12:01,617 --> 01:12:03,285
So you can make this texture?

1907
01:12:03,352 --> 01:12:05,220
He finished making the lobster.

1908
01:12:05,287 --> 01:12:06,221
A minute and 40 seconds.

1909
01:12:06,288 --> 01:12:07,656
Bisque is ready.

1910
01:12:07,723 --> 01:12:10,726
This could make
Jong-won and Do-hee anxious.

1911
01:12:11,694 --> 01:12:14,229
KARINA, it'll be so hard to choose.

1912
01:12:15,431 --> 01:12:16,932
-Oh, no.
-The sauce is already crazy.

1913
01:12:17,366 --> 01:12:19,335
THE ZUCCHINI SHRIMP MOUSSE
COOKED IN THE STEAMER

1914
01:12:19,401 --> 01:12:21,670
Look at the details he added.

1915
01:12:21,737 --> 01:12:24,306
Look at them move as a team.

1916
01:12:24,373 --> 01:12:26,709
-This is what it takes.
-It's incredible.

1917
01:12:26,775 --> 01:12:29,244
This is what it takes
to be in fine dining.

1918
01:12:29,311 --> 01:12:30,713
-Make the base.
-Yes, sir.

1919
01:12:30,779 --> 01:12:34,316
It's such a shame
that she'll have to choose only one team.

1920
01:12:34,383 --> 01:12:35,551
Where's the butter?

1921
01:12:35,617 --> 01:12:36,985
Already in there, sir.

1922
01:12:37,052 --> 01:12:38,153
It's in the box.

1923
01:12:38,787 --> 01:12:40,823
-Here's the bread.
-Steak.

1924
01:12:40,889 --> 01:12:43,359
-Should I cut the bread? Romaine?
-Cut the romaine.

1925
01:12:43,425 --> 01:12:44,493
It's busy here!

1926
01:12:44,560 --> 01:12:47,096
Do-hee cuts lettuce.

1927
01:12:47,162 --> 01:12:48,464
-Do-hee.
-Yes, Chef.

1928
01:12:48,530 --> 01:12:49,832
Make lemonade now.

1929
01:12:49,898 --> 01:12:51,200
It's right here.

1930
01:12:51,266 --> 01:12:52,101
WHEN DID YOU GET IT DONE?

1931
01:12:52,868 --> 01:12:54,603
-Did you add lemon juice?
-I did.

1932
01:12:55,404 --> 01:12:56,405
RETURNS TO HIS TASK

1933
01:12:56,905 --> 01:12:58,807
The lemonade?

1934
01:12:58,874 --> 01:13:01,910
-The lemonade.
-He said he'd add alcohol.

1935
01:13:01,977 --> 01:13:04,079
I love the color.

1936
01:13:04,146 --> 01:13:05,013
Champagne.

1937
01:13:05,080 --> 01:13:07,549
